WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.050
- So, in what some are calling

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.990
the copyright decision of the century

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.610
in Google versus Oracle,

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.060
the Supreme Court has sided with Google.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.060
This is a case that
involved Google copying

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.377
some code related to

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:13.040
Oracle's software in an API.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.290
Now, a lot of people are saying that

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.250
this means APIs are not copyrightable.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.080
That is not actually true.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.730
The Supreme Court didn't
reach that decision.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.390
Instead, it focused on
the more narrow question

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:23.690
of whether Google's use

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.210
of this API code was fair use.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.258
And the Supreme Court said

00:00:27.258 --> 00:00:29.214
that in this particular case,

00:00:29.214 --> 00:00:31.350
this was considered fair use,

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.490
that Google was only using a small part

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.657
of the implementing code that
was used for other purposes,

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:37.910
and went through all the
other fair use factors

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.470
and found in Google's favor.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.890
So we'll see what repercussions this has,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.650
and how this affects the
copyright ability of software.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.160
Will you have to do a fair use analysis

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.030
anytime you take someone else's code?

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:47.863
We'll see,

00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:49.153
and I'll see you in court.

